# Flutter Feature Architecture Pattern

**⚠️ Important: Below there are examples included that may or may not be
applicable to your feature. The goal is to write clean, readable code that can
be tested in isolation.**

- UI should be able to be tested in isolation via widgetbook
- Code is clear and readable
- Keep things as simple as possible, never overengineer. Do not create more
  files than necessary.
- Gain context from the codebase before creating new files

---

When creating or refactoring complex Flutter features, keep in mind this
structure and the key principles. Review requirements and existing code before
deciding the best structure. Always work step by step, getting core pieces in
place before adding more complexity.

## Folder Structure

```
features/
└── feature_name/
    ├── state/
    │   └── feature_notifier.dart     // Interface + Notifier/AsyncNotifier implementation
    ├── views/
    │   ├── feature_view.dart         // Main content view
    │   ├── feature_loading_view.dart // Loading state (only if applicable)
    │   └── feature_error_view.dart   // Error state (only if applicable)
    ├── widgets/
    │   └── complex_widget/           // Use folders for complex widgets
    │       ├── complex_widget.dart   // Public API
    │   └── simple_widget.dart        // Simple widget
    └── feature_page.dart             // Main page (simple coordinator)

data/
└── repositories/
```

## Key Principles For New Pages & Features

1. **Page as Coordinator**: Main page only handles state watching and delegates
   to views
2. **Interface Pattern**: Create abstract interface for notifier for type safety
3. **View Separation**: Only create seperate views when needed.
4. **Private Widget Convention**: Use underscore prefix for private widget files
5. **State Management**: Use Notifier/AsyncNotifier (Riverpod 2.0+ api) without
   riverpod annotations
6. **Generated Models**: Use OpenAPI generated model classes, define APIs in
   repository (in lib/data/repositories/). Repositories are shared and do not
   need to be created for new features.
7. **Specific Props**: Pass specific properties AND specific callbacks to
   widgets. Ideally stateless widgets only import material and theme. Do not
   eagerly create parameters that don't need to change (ie. titles, etc)
8. **Single Responsibility**: Each file/widget has one clear purpose

## Below are examples of how new pages may be implemented depending on requirements. Examples are not exhaustive, but should give you a good idea of how to structure your code.

### Example Pattern 1: AsyncValue.when() - For Data-Dependent UIs

Use when the entire UI depends on async data loading (dashboards, lists, detail
screens):

```dart
class FeaturePage extends ConsumerWidget {
  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context, WidgetRef ref) {
    final feature = ref.watch(featureProvider);
    final notifier = ref.read(featureProvider.notifier);
    
    return Scaffold(
      body: feature.when(
        loading: () => const FeatureLoadingView(),
        error: (error, _) => FeatureErrorView(
          error: error.toString(),
          onRetry: () => ref.invalidate(featureProvider),
        ),
        data: (data) => FeatureView(
          firstName: data.firstName,                    
          isActive: data.isActive,
          onSomething: notifier.performAction,  
        ),
      ),
    );
  }
}
```

### Example Pattern 2: Simple State Conditional - For Forms & Simple Interactions

Use for forms, simple state management, or when loading/error states are handled
inline:

```dart
class BasketPage extends ConsumerWidget {
  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context, WidgetRef ref) {
    final basket = ref.watch(basketProvider);
    final notifier = ref.read(basketProvider.notifier);

    return Scaffold(
      body: basket.isEmpty
          ? BasketEmptyView(
              onStartShopping: () => context.go('/shop'),
            )
          : BasketDataView(
              items: basket.items,
              delivery: basket.deliveryFees,
              subTotal: basket.subTotal,
              onFruitAdded: notifier.addFruit,
              onFruitRemoved: notifier.removeFruit,
              onContinueToShipping: notifier.clear,
            ),
    );
  }
}

// Corresponding simple Notifier (not AsyncNotifier)
class BasketNotifier extends Notifier<Basket> {
  @override
  Basket build() => const Basket.empty();

  void addFruit(Fruit fruit) {
    state = state.copyWithIncreasedQuantity(fruit);
  }

  void clear() {
    state = const Basket.empty();
  }
}
```

## Example Implementation - Notifier Interface

```dart
abstract interface class IFeatureNotifier {
  Future<void> updateSomething(String value);
  void performAction();
}

class FeatureNotifier extends AsyncNotifier<FeatureModel> implements IFeatureNotifier {
  
  @override
  Future<FeatureModel> build() async {
    return ref.read(featureRepositoryProvider).getFeatureData();
  }
  
  @override
  Future<void> updateSomething(String value) async {
    state = const AsyncValue.loading();
    state = await AsyncValue.guard(() async {
      return ref.read(featureRepositoryProvider).updateFeature(value);
    });
  }
}
```

This pattern allows views & widgets to be tested in isolation in widgetbook,
pages go in widgetbook with minimal dependency overrides. Create widgetbook
files for testing UI in widgetbook/ follow the same directory structure as lib/

## Testing & Mocking Guidelines

- Use Mocktail for mocking dependencies and notifiers
- Mock interfaces, not concrete implementations when possible

```dart
// Mock pattern
class MockFeatureNotifier extends Mock implements FeatureNotifier {}

// Provider override pattern
phoneEntryNotifierProvider.overrideWith(MockPhoneEntryNotifier.new)
```

### Widgetbook Examples

Widgetbook is for testing UI in isolation - focus on visual states, not
behavior. Mocks should be very simple. By default, only write one use case for
views and utilize knobs.

#### Page-level Widgetbook (widgetbook/lib/features/feature_name/feature_page.widgetbook.dart)

```dart
@UseCase(name: 'Default', type: FeaturePage)
Widget buildFeaturePageUseCase(BuildContext context) {
  return ProviderScope(
    overrides: [
      featureProvider.overrideWith(() {
        final state = context.knobs.viewState(
          allowedValues: [
            ViewState.loading,
            ViewState.error,
            ViewState.data,
          ],
        );

        return FeatureNotifier(mockData: state == ViewState.data);
      }),
    ],
    child: AppShell(
      activeRoute: AppRoute.feature,
      onRouteTap: (_) {},
      child: const FeaturePage(),
    ),
  );
}
```

#### View-level Widgetbook (widgetbook/lib/features/feature_name/views/feature_view.widgetbook.dart)

```dart
@UseCase(
  name: 'Default',
  type: FeatureView,
)
Widget buildFeatureViewUseCase(BuildContext context) {
  return FeatureView(
    title: context.knobs.string(
      label: 'Title',
      initialValue: 'Feature Title',
    ),
    isActive: context.knobs.boolean(
      label: 'Is Active',
      initialValue: true,
    ),
    onSomething: () {},
  );
}
```

